---
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Visual from '../components/blocks/Visual.astro';

// Drop a photo at public/images/about.jpg (or .png / .webp / .gif).
const portraitSrc = [
  '/images/about.jpg',
  '/images/about.png',
  '/images/about.webp',
  '/images/about.gif',
].find((src) => fs.existsSync(path.join(process.cwd(), 'public', src.slice(1))));

// Right-column copy — edit anytime. Separate paragraphs with a blank line.
const about = `
I can trace my career in product design back to the early days of MySpace, when I first learned I could customize code to make a Death Cab for Cutie song play when friends landed on my page. It felt like magic. I couldn’t put words to what intrigued me about the internet and being creative with digital tools, but I was obsessed.

Fast forward to my first decade as a working designer, unsure where to look to fulfill my curiosity about how to build a career doing this cool thing. In hindsight, that decade was about exposure. I worked in pharma advertising while still in school, learning how creativity works inside heavy regulation. I joined Happy Cog to understand craft from people who literally wrote the standards for the web. I spent two years at Angela Duckworth's Character Lab figuring out how research translates into practice and learning firsthand what sustained effort toward long-term goals actually looks like. I designed for millions of students and teachers at College Board during a global pandemic, which taught me what high-stakes product work actually feels like. And I led Fortune 500 engagements at Think Company, learning how to sell design to stakeholders who didn’t always understand our value outside of ‘making things look pretty’. Each move exposed me to a new corner of the industry.

In 2022, I stopped sampling and started building. I joined Northwestern Mutual to lead design for the platform our financial advisors use to manage $300B+ in client assets, and I've spent the last four years scaling that work: growing the team to eight people, expanding from one product domain to owning experience strategy across an entire portfolio, and earning a promotion to Assistant Director. Around the same time, I started teaching at Penn, where I co-created the university's first UX/UI course. Teaching 200+ students has made me a better manager and managing a real team has made me a better teacher. 

I'm drawn to complexity. I genuinely enjoy making sense of big organizations, messy problems, and high stakes. And I'm convinced that the best way to navigate complexity is to have seen it from a dozen different angles first, then stay long enough to apply what you've learned.
`.trim();

const paragraphs = about.split(/\n\s*\n/);
---

<BaseLayout title="About — Tom McQuaid" current="about">
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">About</h1>
  <div class="home">
    <div class="home__portrait">
      <Visual src={portraitSrc} alt="Tom McQuaid" />
    </div>
    <div class="home__intro prose">
      {paragraphs.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
